A||N||5||David Bartlett||Service Engine - Code 32 EGR||||1||
Z||000000||David Bartlett||09-29-2001||02:17 AM||||Alright, I've been getting a Service Engine Soon light -  Code 32 while driving around 120km on the Highway. It only comes on then. At one time it would only come on 1 in 4 trips. Now its on 3 in 4 trips and I've decided to fix the problem.  Is it the valve or solenoid? Is there a way to do a check? Will it do harm by driving with it improperly working?||||reg||1
Z||000001||Fformula88||09-29-2001||09:43 AM||||Might not be either.  Remember, the reason the engine sets the code is because it senses a vacumn problem in the EGR system.  I'd say chances are its a vacumn leak from a cracked or broken line.  Could be the valve or soleniod too of course.<P>Check all the plastic vacumn lines and fittings for any cracks or breaks.  Be careful, they get really brittle with age!<P>Next check the EGR tube.  On V6's, it runs from the EGR valve to the bottom of the intake plenum.  This is a common part that cracks and causes a vacumn leak resulting in CEL-32.  Hope this helps some.  ||||reg||1
Z||000002||David Bartlett||09-29-2001||11:48 AM||||I don't think its the tube because it looks new. It's all shinny and gold. If it were the EGR tube wouldn't my headers glow red? <P>I've read in one of the posts that you can jump the A & B connections to the solinoid and see if the engine light goes away. Anyone try this?||||reg||1
Z||000003||Two88GTs||09-29-2001||09:49 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by David Bartlett:<BR><B>I don't think its the tube because it looks new. It's all shinny and gold. If it were the EGR tube wouldn't my headers glow red? <P>I've read in one of the posts that you can jump the A & B connections to the solinoid and see if the engine light goes away. Anyone try this?</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Let me guess, your car runs great otherwise?<BR>No high idle at startup, right?  One of my 88GTs was like that when I bought it, and turned out to be the EGR valve solenoid.  The EGR valve solenoid is the most expensive part to replace for the code 32 problem but probably the easiest to replace.  Thankfully, a junkyard unit($2) solved my problem.||||reg||1
Z||000004||David Bartlett||09-30-2001||01:39 AM||||Yep, I figure thats the trouble. I think I'll look around at the junkyards for one and see if i can fix my old one.||||reg||1
Z||000005||mrfiero||09-30-2001||06:19 PM||||There is a rubber vacuum line that runs from the solenoid to the firewall (where it ataches to a metal line).  Over time this hose cracks or completely falls off.  Before you replace the solenoid remove and replace this vacuum line (even if it looks OK).  Buy about 18" (I believe it is 7/32) and trim to fit. <P>Then if that doesn't solve the problem replace the solenoid.  I personally have never had a solenoid go bad on me, so that is why I doubt it is the culprit.<P>Don't forget to clear the codes before driving it again....disconnect the battery for about 15 seconds then reattach.  Jumping the A & B terminals of the ALDL just tells you what codes ae stored.....it won't clear them.<P>Good luck.||||reg||1
